window.docContents[142].resolve({"/Gamma-Function/___5___1-Special-Notation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___1-Special-Notation--___5___1-Special-Notation--Variables-and-parameters":{"contents":"The source convention is\nj,m,n\\ \\text{are nonnegative integers},\\qquad k\\ \\text{is a nonnegative integer except in §5.20},\nx,y\\in\\mathbb R,\\qquad z=x+\\mathrm{i}y,\\qquad a,b,q,s,w\\in\\mathbb R\\ \\text{or}\\ \\mathbb C,\\qquad |q|<1.Thus j, m, n, and the usual Chapter 5 k-indices are naturally represented by\nℕ; the stated §5.20 exception is a local convention of that later section.  Real\ncoordinates determine a complex argument through z = x + i y.  When a parameter is\nallowed to be real or complex, the formal development uses ℂ and inserts the canonical\nreal-to-complex coercion.  The q restriction is a norm inequality in the complex model.\n\n\\delta>0,\\qquad \\delta\\ \\text{arbitrarily small}.The symbol δ is an arbitrary small positive constant.  Its use is local to a\nstatement—typically as a sector margin or an error tolerance—and means δ > 0, with\nthe intended choice made as small as needed.  The elementary formal content is that every\npositive tolerance admits such a choice:\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function\u0009§5.1 Special Notation\u0009§5.1 Special Notation","header":"Variables and parameters","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___1-Special-Notation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___1-Special-Notation--___5___1-Special-Notation--Variables-and-parameters"}});